      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t}+2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{t}-2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{t}-2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{t}-2}{\\sqrt{t}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{t(\\sqrt{t}-2)}{(\\sqrt{t}+2)(\\sqrt{t}-2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t(\\sqrt{t}-2)}{(\\sqrt{t})^{2}-{2}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{t(\\sqrt{t}-2)}{t-4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
